		<td class="row2" width="100%" height="28" valign="top"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
			<tr>
				<td width="100%"><a href="viewtopic.php?p=9129&amp;sid=8d6c0a228fcdcb196236f9adf71e85c3#9129"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/icon_minipost.gif" width="12" height="9" alt="Post" title="Post" border="0" /></a><span class="postdetails">Posted: Tue Jul 02, 2002 10:09 am<span class="gen">&nbsp;</span>&nbsp; &nbsp;Post subject: </span></td>
				<td valign="top" align="right" nowrap="nowrap"><a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=9129&amp;sid=8d6c0a228fcdcb196236f9adf71e85c3"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/lang_english/icon_quote.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>   </td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">In the cool dark of the library, the shadows punctuated by a wisp of faerie light here and there, Lamaridine pored through yet another thick book. The dark circles underneath her eyes were the only indication of her obstinance in finding the answer to her question. She patiently flipped through the pages, her growing frustation not showing a bit as she knew, yet again the answer was not before her. It never was, and probably never would be. But what she had learned in the past few months… most of it not from the books themselves, but from the conversations around her as she became more of a fixture within the rooms than a person studying. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
<i> “Bah,”</i> she thought to herself, <i> “There are some things that cannot exist in the books of man or elf. This has been nothing more than a snipe hunt, and I’m more the fool for trying to track it down like this.” </i>
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
What was she looking for? Even she had problems saying anymore. Certainly not help from the guild that she had been blessed with. The excuses mounted, they were far too busy, had their own problems, shouldn’t be troubled that their leader was becoming far too distracted with what had started out such a small simple question. Really, though, what underlined it all was a simple stubborn sense of duty. This involved her family, and therefore was out of bounds for any one else to deal with. If they could not handle it, it could not be helped. It was as simple as that. She shook her head as she considered once again turning to them, as she had advised so many others to do. No, this had become more than a simple family affair, and should not concern anyone not of Koada’dal blood. And as well meaning as they were, once she mentioned it to one, it would be with them all, and the ones that did not fail to understand the implications would be well inclined to tell others, and the ones that didn’t see the significance would have no reason to let their lips stray.  No, the shame was born once upon the shoulders of her people, and would not be again if she could help it. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
She sighed, and was about to shut the book and call it a night when her eyes settled upon one, small entry in the handwritten book, the strokes of the ancient writing seeming to indicate that it was written more as an afterthought  than an official statement. Her eyes grew wide as she read the passage, and then re-read it, blinking hard at the apparent truth in front of her. Re-energized, she marked her place and slammed the book shut. Her stride marked with terrible purpose she left the library, book in hand with these words reverberating in her head, <i> “Tunare help us if they’re right.”</i>
<br />

<br />
 
<br />

<br />
