			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">“Tynkale!” the voice shouted with urgency as it’s owner came rushing through the halls towards the center room. The younger of the two elves turned towards the source of the call, eyes alert and watchful. A very young boy came running up, dressed in training blues, a new recruit from the looks of him. “They told me..” he paused huffing and puffing, out of breath from the run, “They told me to talk to you, that I should tell you what happened.” He stopped, hands on his knees as he gulped in the air, suddenly realizing to whom he was speaking. Tynkale, for his part, folded his arms and waited patiently for the boy to catch his breath, almost smiling if not for the import of the message. It might be something, it might not… time would tell. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
“What’s your name, recruit?” the trainer began.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
“Nishran, sir,” the boy replied quickly, standing straight.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
“Well then, Nishran, what did you see? What happened?”
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
“My friend Sirenae and I were walking back to our rooms when… I know we’re not supposed to be out after hours, sir, but well… anyway, we were walking by the dorms where all of the clerics are put and for the briefest of seconds,” he paused for a moment, trying to think of how to put it, and looking slightly embarrassed, “Well, we saw, or maybe it was just me.. anyway, we saw this kind of purply light behind the building. I say purple but it wasn’t really purple, and it wasn’t really light. I wouldn’tve hardly noticed it in the dark, except that for a few seconds there I, or we, couldn’t see the light off the edges of the trees… well, you know how there’s a bunch of lights over on the other side of the building,” Nishran explained. Tynkale nodded, “In the courtyard, yes, I’m familiar with them. Continue.”  
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
“We’ve always been able to see the light through the trees along that side of the building. Except for the few seconds where it was well.. it just was gone, and there was this kind of… not even darkness, really, I don’t think we would have noticed that, but this kind of .. well, anti-light that felt purple but really wasn’t. It kind of swooped up from the lights, near as we can tell and then just as suddenly it was gone.”
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
“And then what happened?” Tynkale replied, carefully measured.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
“Umm… well.. Sirenae was curious, and she’s far braver than I, so we snuck over to the courtyard. I’m not sure you’ll like this, sir, but there’s something wrong there… the trees, I can’t quite put my finger on it, but there’s something wrong with them. They have all of their branches and bark and leaves, but I know.. I know there’s something not right with them. Maybe you’ll be able to see it if you looked, but I can’t explain it. It doesn’t feel right there.” Nishran shook his head and squeezed his eyes shut, clearing his thoughts, “Anyway, they told me to come and tell you, that you would want to know. I’m sorry we were out after curfew.” He took a step back, and bowed deeply.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Tynkale, showing a calm demeanor that he certainly didn’t have inwardly, nodded sternly, “Nishran, I want you and Sirenae to report to my offices first thing at daybreak this morning. I have some… additional duties to add to your roster. Breaking curfew, especially at a time like this, is certainly not acceptable, despite what information you may have uncovered. But for now, return to your room, and I will speak with you both then.” He dismissed the young elf, who looked a bit lost at being turned loose with so little to show for it. The trainer barely registered it in his mind as he turned over the possibilities in his head of what the boy had witnessed. This wasn’t exactly what they had been told to look out for, and that bothered him more than words could express.  
<br />

<br />
