			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">The echoes of memories flitting around the edges of her vision, Lamaridine ran quickly through the streets of Felwithe, bound for the outer recesses of town, her eyes only aware of what obstacles to avoid and how. They had played around them and never noticed. They had made up ghastly stories about them and never thought they might be true. They had grown up with them in their lives, and that was the most disturbing part of it all. To know what she knew now, and to know that the trees and statues themselves had lent their influence, however slight, to the person she was today, filled her with such a revulsion that she could barely stand upright. And to think that they may have had a more direct persuasion was beyond what she was willing to accept, even tentatively. 
<br />

<br />
Winded by her sprinting she slowed as she approached the house, it's outline low in the horizon, and barely lit from the inside, it's walls and windows looking almost passed over, forgotten and dusty. It was little more than a trick of the light, Lamaridine knew, as it was intended to be. Once at it's doorstep the dignity and diligence this house was kept with was more than apparent, it was overwhelming. She raised the knocker on the door, seeing lights on the inside. 
<br />

<br />
A few moments later, a dour faced elf answered the door, his thin lips almost ground into non-existence from an almost constant frown. Seeing him almost always set Lamaridine on edge, and this time was no different. &quot;Umm, hello Sir Abviel, is Qallain in this eve? I have something rather.. important to discuss with him.&quot; 
<br />

<br />
With a raised eyebrow and an instant deepening of the perpetual scowl, Qallain's father nodded curtly, exactly once, with exactly enough of a movement to recognize it as a nod, no more, no less. &quot;Do come in, Lamaridine, and we'll see if he's taking visitors.&quot; Trying not to shudder with the acid edge to the tone Lamaridine stepped inside of the immaculate house. &quot;Saraphine,&quot; he called roughly to his wife, &quot;Rouse Qallain from whatever it is that he's doing. He's got a visitor and likely she won't be staying long.&quot; Glancing over Lamaridine as if she were clutter in his perfect home, Abviel walked back to his study, ignoring anything that might stay him in his path.
<br />

<br />
 
<br />

<br />
