				<td width="100%"><a href="viewtopic.php?p=9194&amp;sid=8d6c0a228fcdcb196236f9adf71e85c3#9194"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/icon_minipost.gif" width="12" height="9" alt="Post" title="Post" border="0" /></a><span class="postdetails">Posted: Sat Aug 17, 2002 1:09 pm<span class="gen">&nbsp;</span>&nbsp; &nbsp;Post subject: </span></td>
				<td valign="top" align="right" nowrap="nowrap"><a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=9194&amp;sid=8d6c0a228fcdcb196236f9adf71e85c3"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/lang_english/icon_quote.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>   </td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">A short dry laugh escapes Lamaridine's mouth before she's able to think better of it. &quot;A cure? Nay, but mayhaps an explanation. That -is- what I started looking for, a cure... Me in my arrogance thinking I could heal what the most gifted clerics in Felwithe had no answer for.&quot;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
She again glances around, tucking a lock of hair behind her ear absentmindedly. Looking sharply into the darkness, she relaxes slightly, holding her tense position. She hands a thick well worn book, silver-leafed and dog eared at the edges, over to her friend. Qallain instantly sees the bookmark, but hesitates before opening the tome to the spot. &quot;What is this, Lamaridine?&quot;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;It's a book, of course,&quot; she smiles at the half-hearted attempt at a joke. &quot;Best I explain from the beginning before you read what I found though. Oh, where to begin...&quot; she sighs, pausing in thought. &quot;My mother will do. She's been getting better, of a sorts. She's awake more anyway.&quot; Lamaridine's eyes narrow as she folds her arms around her waist, &quot;But she has... episodes.. She's not.. right, not normal. Sometimes it doesn't happen for days, but then suddenly... she'll see things, and sometimes she tells whoever's listening what she sees.&quot;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;And that is...?&quot; Qallain coaxes, trying to coax the words out of her.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Lamaridine frowns, &quot;That's just it.. it makes no sense. She sees shadows everywhere, even on the brightest of days. She sees great wars, man, dwarf and elf alike falling to an army of shades. She sees a powerful malignant... force coming from Thex's castle itself. She once saw Tunare, but in chains to elves bearing the king's standard, the Goddess Herself bloodied and battered. She sounds like a madwoman at times, Qallain, and heaven help me for saying so about my own mother. But there's one thing she -says- that really bothers me...&quot;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;She says we brought it on ourselves, that what will come to pass stems from no other source than another Koada'dal arrogance, that it's roots are deep in our blood.&quot; Lamaridine quiets for a moment, gauging the expression on Qallain's face. To any other, it would have appeared impassive, stoic, but she could discern the slight twitch of suprise and concern barely etched on his face. Satisfied, she continues,&quot;Do you know how we came to be here in Felwithe? After the dwarves granted us sanctuary? Of the first years here, establishing the city, after we all beat the orcs back into their little corner? I didn't, it was never taught to me, just a little period of history that somehow got skipped over in my lessons. Did they educate you better, Qallain?&quot;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
 
<br />

<br />
 <i></i></span><span class="gentblsmall"></span></td>
