				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Lamaridine sighs, &quot;Aye, I know, I know... I've been avoiding the issue. I'll hope by the end of this telling you'll forgive me for that.&quot; She pauses, once more scanning her surroundings, thinking to herself that paranoia doesn't seem that far fetched these days...
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;I'm a pretty straightforward woman, you know that, Qallain, so I'll start at the beginning of what I've learned. Takish'Hiz blah blah, the burning prince, blah blah, we had to look for a new home. Dwarves, Faydwer, orcs, you know the story there. It's the time right after the orcs that I'm worried about, and that I think is still having ramifications today. Tell me, Qallain, when is the last time you saw Delwynd?&quot; 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Qallain thought for a moment, frowned slightly and tugged on the sleeves of his perfectly kept robes as if warding off a chill. &quot;I'd thought he was studying with the clerics in Felwithe, and what does that have to do with anything in the first place?&quot;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;Well, at first I thought nothing of it like you,&quot; Lamaridine replied, her eyes carefully controlled, &quot;but I'm not so sure now...&quot; She waved her hands in front of her in a quick rough gesture, &quot;But more on that later, you want to know what I've been beating around the bush about. Alright, here goes...
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;As you know, several months ago, there was another room found in the library at the academy. I think they were planning on expanding, but whatever the reason, and I'm just paranoid enough at this point to believe that that wall being torn down wasn't an accident.... I was already searching for an answer to my mother's condition, or at least a reliable interpretation of her visions, when I came upon a very old text. Actually, they were all very old, but this one appeared to be the grandfather of even those ancient books. It was written in the old tongue, and honestly I knew very little of it at that point, but something struck me about the book... fine, fine, moving on...&quot; She responded to the weary look on Qallain's face. &quot;I hired someone to do a translation for me, and it spoke of the time right after we settled in Felwithe. Apparently, we were not the first settlers here. Someone else had been here first, there were tunnels and carved out caverns, and in fact some of them still exist underneath the city. Any trace of the former occupants was obliterated by the time we got here, either by the orcs or by some other force. It might not seem important, but it is something to bear in mind in the rest of this....
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;So, I read on, and other books in that room. We were a very frightened lot with our numbers so decimated by the time we made it to that small corner. We were trying to survive, and in doing so, we may have made one single fatal mistake. It all started shortly after King Thex's great great grandfather took the throne.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;The new king and his wife trusted their advisors, there is no doubt of that. So much so that when the threat of the orcs renewed and their forces seemed to double, and more than that, grow that much more canny in their tactics, so much so that the king met with his advisors and asked them to come up with a permanent solution to the issue. There were five advisors, a cleric, a paladin, and all of the arcane arts were represented, all with the best interests of the kingdom in mind. And they had glorious arguments about how to best protect their tiny fledgling kingdom, and worse whether they should turn from Tunare's grace to do it. Some argued that She had forsaken us in Takish'Hiz, and it was only fitting that we strike it out on our own. One in particular, a mage named Buadach, was very outspoken in this view. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;Buadach suggested something one evening, and I rather imagine it was like this one,&quot; Lamaridine continued, gesturing toward the night sky. She shuddered for a brief second and looked over to her friend, knowing that even he, agnostic that he was, would not appreciate what was forthcoming. &quot;He suggested that the powers that be in Felwithe do nothing more, and nothing less than summon a powerful spirit and bind it to the walls of the city, nay to it's very foundation. The others, even those that agreed with Buadach, thought this was too extreme an answer. But what none of them realized is that he was already working toward that ends. And what even Buadach didn't realize is that what he was summoning wasn't a simple spirit, mindless and dull, like so many of his other pets. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
The five presented their plans to the king and his wife. Most were some variation of fortifying Kelethin's defenses. Make no mistake that there is a sure reason why more Fier'dal roam Norrath than Koada'dal. If a body cares about their home and are encouraged to be there, the more likely they are to want to protect it. Not very nice, but practical... In any case, Buadach had asked to present his case on his own, not wanting to bother with a trifling commitee who didn't understand his genius. And present his case he did, but only as a matter of fact, not one of choice.&quot; The paladin eyes grew cold and narrowed to slits. She choked the next words out, barely able to prevent the acid tone in her voice from overwhelming her. &quot;He had already summoned the creature, and was in the process of binding it to every single brick that makes up this city. He was almost mad with the power this spiked through his veins, and I'm sure he thought that the others would be pleased, but I'm sure you can at least hazard a guess to their reaction.&quot;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
She sighed, and looked out over the city, in this spot, the one place that she had <i>  always</i> felt entirely safe. She acknowledged to herself that it still did, here anyway. &quot;And, well... they've spent a good number of years trying to undo what Buadach did. You see, the problem was that by the time they decided on how to handle what he had done, he was too far in his madness to tell them how to undo it. And the spirit, well... from what little I can find on it, it's from another.. place. Maybe like what Rithnok and Aldezworr have spoken of, other dimensions and worlds... I don't really concern myself with the where. What I do know is that it's still here, consumed with an anger and hatred I'm sure Innoruuk would have to admire, and Qallain, I think it was dormant. I think it was sleeping, or something very like that, and now... well, now it's not.&quot; She looked to her friend, very unlike the regal and rigid posture he'd unconsciously grown so used to out of her. The woman before him appeared tired, confused and ragged. &quot;Now, I'm not sure.. that could be because they were managing to free it, and now it's back or it's stuck again. I can't find any reference to anything about it past two generations ago. Maybe they forgot about it, but it hasn't forgotten about us... and Qallain, she's right.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;It is our own arrogance.&quot;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
 
<br />
